1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,788
Lepakshi's famous hanging pillar,

2
00:00:01,788 --> 00:00:04,290
is actually not fully in the air.

3
00:00:04,290 --> 00:00:08,580
An inspection found that only its corner touches the ground.

4
00:00:08,580 --> 00:00:12,155
Still, people pass cloth under it.

5
00:00:12,155 --> 00:00:15,730
So is this a miracle, a mistake, or brilliant engineering?

6
00:00:15,730 --> 00:00:21,273
In Lepakshi village in Andhra Pradesh stands the Veerabhadra temple.

7
00:00:21,273 --> 00:00:24,044
In its hall of pillars,

8
00:00:24,044 --> 00:00:27,924
the other pillars stand holding up the roof.

9
00:00:27,924 --> 00:00:32,358
But among them, this pillar looks different.

10
00:00:32,358 --> 00:00:37,346
People say this pillar hangs in the air,

11
00:00:37,346 --> 00:00:41,780
as if someone has lifted it up.

12
00:00:41,780 --> 00:00:44,655
In this video we will go along with the evidence.

13
00:00:44,655 --> 00:00:47,529
First, we will see what the scripture says.

14
00:00:47,529 --> 00:00:49,685
Then we will see what the inspection found,

15
00:00:49,685 --> 00:00:51,841
where the experts disagree,

16
00:00:51,841 --> 00:00:55,075
and what nobody knows yet. At the end,

17
00:00:55,075 --> 00:00:57,590
what you can see when you go there.

18
00:00:57,590 --> 00:01:01,523
The story begins with the Skanda Purana. Purana means an ancient scripture,

19
00:01:01,523 --> 00:01:04,741
in which stories of gods, holy places and sites are written.

20
00:01:04,741 --> 00:01:07,602
This scripture mentions the name of Lepakshi,

21
00:01:07,602 --> 00:01:10,104
and it mentions it with great respect.

22
00:01:10,104 --> 00:01:13,680
This is where the holiness of this place began.

23
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:17,361
According to the Skanda Purana, Lepakshi is one of the divyakshetras.

24
00:01:17,361 --> 00:01:19,406
Divyakshetra means a sacred land

25
00:01:19,406 --> 00:01:22,679
where the special presence of the deity is believed to be.

26
00:01:22,679 --> 00:01:27,178
This place is counted among the important pilgrimage sites of Lord Shiva.

27
00:01:27,178 --> 00:01:30,450
That is why devotees have been coming here for centuries.

28
00:01:30,450 --> 00:01:34,494
This temple is dedicated to Veerabhadra. In the stories of the Puranas, Veerabhadra

29
00:01:34,494 --> 00:01:37,068
is considered the fierce form of Shiv.

30
00:01:37,068 --> 00:01:40,377
In the sanctum, a stone idol is seen from far away,

31
00:01:40,377 --> 00:01:43,686
in the light of a lamp. Devotees stand with folded hands,

32
00:01:43,686 --> 00:01:46,260
and the air smells of incense sticks.

33
00:01:46,260 --> 00:01:49,195
There is a story behind the village's name too.

34
00:01:49,195 --> 00:01:50,663
A folk tale says,

35
00:01:50,663 --> 00:01:53,598
that the wounded bird Jatayu from the Ramayan fell here.

36
00:01:53,598 --> 00:01:57,634
Shri Ram said, 'Le Pakshi', meaning 'rise, bird'. That very word,

37
00:01:57,634 --> 00:02:01,670
they say, later became the name of this village, Lepakshi.

38
00:02:01,670 --> 00:02:05,804
Now let's come to Virupanna, who got the temple built.

39
00:02:05,804 --> 00:02:07,871
According to folk tale,

40
00:02:07,871 --> 00:02:12,005
the king's ministers accused him.

41
00:02:12,005 --> 00:02:18,206
The charge was that they had wasted royal treasury money on the temple.

42
00:02:18,206 --> 00:02:21,823
This charge, the story says, was false,

43
00:02:21,823 --> 00:02:25,440
and was made on wrong advice.

44
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:26,965
The story goes on to say,

45
00:02:26,965 --> 00:02:31,158
that guessing the punishment, Virupanna took out his own eyes.

46
00:02:31,158 --> 00:02:34,207
He threw them against the temple wall.

47
00:02:34,207 --> 00:02:38,019
Even today, red marks are shown on the wall.

48
00:02:38,019 --> 00:02:41,450
People call these the marks of Virupanna's eyes.

49
00:02:41,450 --> 00:02:45,526
Now let's move from the story to the proof. The question is simple,

50
00:02:45,526 --> 00:02:48,490
is this pillar really hanging in the air?

51
00:02:48,490 --> 00:02:51,824
For that, we have to look at the pillar carefully.

52
00:02:51,824 --> 00:02:55,900
And understand how this temple and its roof were built.

53
00:02:55,900 --> 00:02:58,751
This temple belongs to the time of the Vijayanagara empire.

54
00:02:58,751 --> 00:03:02,670
Vijayanagara, that is, the big empire of South India, whose capital was Hampi.

55
00:03:02,670 --> 00:03:06,234
The craftsmen of this empire were famous for their stone work.

56
00:03:06,234 --> 00:03:10,510
Their temples are still known today for their fine carving.

57
00:03:10,510 --> 00:03:12,187
The pillar is made of granite.

58
00:03:12,187 --> 00:03:14,534
Granite, that is, a very hard and heavy stone,

59
00:03:14,534 --> 00:03:16,881
which is found plentifully in South India.

60
00:03:16,881 --> 00:03:20,235
Cutting and shaping this stone was not an easy job.

61
00:03:20,235 --> 00:03:22,247
And when the stone is this heavy,

62
00:03:22,247 --> 00:03:25,600
it seems even harder to keep it standing in the air.

63
00:03:25,600 --> 00:03:27,411
This pillar is in the mandap.

64
00:03:27,411 --> 00:03:29,946
A mandap is an open hall resting on pillars,

65
00:03:29,946 --> 00:03:32,119
where people would sit and festivals would be held.

66
00:03:32,119 --> 00:03:35,378
In this mandap at Lepakshi, there are many pillars.

67
00:03:35,378 --> 00:03:38,637
The pillars have carvings of dancers, musicians and gods,

68
00:03:38,637 --> 00:03:40,810
which look alive.

69
00:03:40,810 --> 00:03:45,940
Now the most important proof. When the pillar was physically examined,

70
00:03:45,940 --> 00:03:50,043
that is, it was measured and looked at closely,

71
00:03:50,043 --> 00:03:55,686
the picture changed. The pillar is not completely in the air.

72
00:03:55,686 --> 00:03:59,789
Its corner lightly touches the ground.

73
00:03:59,789 --> 00:04:03,380
This is the first key to this secret.

74
00:04:03,380 --> 00:04:05,950
Think, when you pull a cloth out from below,

75
00:04:05,950 --> 00:04:09,161
it slips out from under most of the part.

76
00:04:09,161 --> 00:04:11,410
That is why the pillar seems to be hanging.

77
00:04:11,410 --> 00:04:14,943
But at the corner, the contact between the stone and the ground remains.

78
00:04:14,943 --> 00:04:16,870
The illusion comes from here.

79
00:04:16,870 --> 00:04:19,897
The Archaeological Survey of India, that is, ASI,

80
00:04:19,897 --> 00:04:23,932
takes care of the country's old monuments.

81
00:04:23,932 --> 00:04:28,976
The Veerabhadra temple at Lepakshi is also under ASI protection.

82
00:04:28,976 --> 00:04:33,516
That is why breaking or moving the pillar is not allowed today.

83
00:04:33,516 --> 00:04:38,560
Even the investigation is done very carefully, without causing any damage.

84
00:04:38,560 --> 00:04:42,827
So the biggest proof is that the pillar does not defy gravity.

85
00:04:42,827 --> 00:04:44,140
Gravity means that pull

86
00:04:44,140 --> 00:04:47,094
which draws everything towards the ground.

87
00:04:47,094 --> 00:04:48,736
The pillar does touch the ground,

88
00:04:48,736 --> 00:04:51,690
just so slightly that the eye gets fooled.

89
00:04:51,690 --> 00:04:56,948
Now the question is, if the pillar does not carry the whole load,

90
00:04:56,948 --> 00:04:59,577
then how does the roof stay up?

91
00:04:59,577 --> 00:05:03,258
The answer is hidden in the structure of the roof.

92
00:05:03,258 --> 00:05:06,413
The roof of this mandap is corbelled,

93
00:05:06,413 --> 00:05:09,568
and built using the dry-stone method.

94
00:05:09,568 --> 00:05:14,300
Let us understand these terms in simple language.

95
00:05:14,300 --> 00:05:16,353
Corbel means stone over stone,

96
00:05:16,353 --> 00:05:20,118
with each layer sticking out a little further. Layer after layer like this,

97
00:05:20,118 --> 00:05:22,514
the stones come and meet in the middle.

98
00:05:22,514 --> 00:05:25,594
This way, instead of the roof's weight going straight down,

99
00:05:25,594 --> 00:05:27,990
it gets spread out to the supports on the sides.

100
00:05:27,990 --> 00:05:32,012
Dry-stone means stones joined without mortar or cement.

101
00:05:32,012 --> 00:05:36,437
No lime, no mortar, just stone sitting properly on stone.

102
00:05:36,437 --> 00:05:39,655
The craftsmen carved the stones so finely

103
00:05:39,655 --> 00:05:44,080
that they locked into each other by their own weight.

104
00:05:44,080 --> 00:05:45,163
Now think,

105
00:05:45,163 --> 00:05:52,202
all the pillars of the mandap work together like a connected web.

106
00:05:52,202 --> 00:05:57,617
The roof above spreads the load across all of them.

107
00:05:57,617 --> 00:06:01,408
That is why if one pillar moves a little,

108
00:06:01,408 --> 00:06:05,740
it does not bring down the whole roof. If the web is strong,

109
00:06:05,740 --> 00:06:09,530
even a weak link stays in place.

110
00:06:09,530 --> 00:06:14,711
This is the real brilliance of the Vijayanagara craftsmen.

111
00:06:14,711 --> 00:06:19,892
They did not have today's computers or machines.

112
00:06:19,892 --> 00:06:26,800
Still, they built a structure that spreads the load evenly.

113
00:06:26,800 --> 00:06:30,830
The hanging pillar is a glimpse of this structure,

114
00:06:30,830 --> 00:06:34,860
which still amazes us today.

115
00:06:34,860 --> 00:06:38,494
So how must it have been built? The most likely method is this.

116
00:06:38,494 --> 00:06:40,674
First, the pillars were raised,

117
00:06:40,674 --> 00:06:43,945
then the beams and the roof stones were placed on top.

118
00:06:43,945 --> 00:06:47,942
When the roof was fully joined, the load was shared across the whole web.

119
00:06:47,942 --> 00:06:53,030
Then even if a pillar lifts slightly from below, the roof does not fall.

120
00:06:53,030 --> 00:06:56,337
Now comes the most interesting part.

121
00:06:56,337 --> 00:07:00,196
Why is the pillar slightly above the ground?

122
00:07:00,196 --> 00:07:04,606
Experts have different opinions on this.

123
00:07:04,606 --> 00:07:09,567
And each side has its own argument. Let's hear

124
00:07:09,567 --> 00:07:15,080
both views with the evidence, without rushing to a decision.

125
00:07:15,080 --> 00:07:20,598
Some experts say it was made this way on purpose.

126
00:07:20,598 --> 00:07:23,608
The craftsmen wanted to show off their skill.

127
00:07:23,608 --> 00:07:28,624
They wanted to show that their roof is so strong

128
00:07:28,624 --> 00:07:33,139
that the pillar can stay up even without full support.

129
00:07:33,139 --> 00:07:36,650
So it's a wonder that was built with careful planning.

130
00:07:36,650 --> 00:07:38,840
Other experts say something different.

131
00:07:38,840 --> 00:07:43,220
According to them, the pillar was fully resting on the ground at first.

132
00:07:43,220 --> 00:07:47,600
Then over time, the ground below sank unevenly.

133
00:07:47,600 --> 00:07:49,790
This is called foundation settlement,

134
00:07:49,790 --> 00:07:51,980
meaning the ground slowly sinking.

135
00:07:51,980 --> 00:07:55,055
This side has more arguments too.

136
00:07:55,055 --> 00:07:58,515
Light earthquake tremors, called seismic tremors,

137
00:07:58,515 --> 00:08:00,822
must have come many times over the centuries.

138
00:08:00,822 --> 00:08:05,050
Such tremors can slowly move even heavy stones.

139
00:08:05,050 --> 00:08:08,510
Maybe that's how the pillar slid from its place.

140
00:08:08,510 --> 00:08:11,898
So the evidence is the same, but the ways of reading it are different.

141
00:08:11,898 --> 00:08:14,608
Some people believe it was the craftsmen's intention.

142
00:08:14,608 --> 00:08:17,657
Some believe it was the effect of time and the ground.

143
00:08:17,657 --> 00:08:22,400
So far, no side has been able to completely prove the others wrong.

144
00:08:22,400 --> 00:08:26,557
And we won't give any verdict here. You decide for yourself,

145
00:08:26,557 --> 00:08:29,882
would such skilled craftsmen leave this behind by mistake?

146
00:08:29,882 --> 00:08:34,455
Or did centuries of ground movement and tremors play their game?

147
00:08:34,455 --> 00:08:37,365
Each answer is interesting in itself,

148
00:08:37,365 --> 00:08:40,690
and each answer shows the hard work of the craftsmen.

149
00:08:40,690 --> 00:08:43,807
Now, the story that is told the most.

150
00:08:43,807 --> 00:08:45,884
They say that in the British era,

151
00:08:45,884 --> 00:08:48,654
a British engineer came to solve this mystery.

152
00:08:48,654 --> 00:08:53,156
He wanted to find out what was really holding up the roof.

153
00:08:53,156 --> 00:08:55,580
And he grabbed hold of the pillar itself.

154
00:08:55,580 --> 00:08:57,314
According to folk tale,

155
00:08:57,314 --> 00:09:01,214
he tried to remove or shake the pillar.

156
00:09:01,214 --> 00:09:03,815
The pillar slid from its place,

157
00:09:03,815 --> 00:09:08,582
and since then it has been hanging a little slanted. The story says,

158
00:09:08,582 --> 00:09:13,350
the roof started shaking and the engineer got scared and stopped the work.

159
00:09:13,350 --> 00:09:14,585
But please note,

160
00:09:14,585 --> 00:09:18,290
no solid proof of this story has been found.

161
00:09:18,290 --> 00:09:22,407
Whether any engineer really pierced the pillar or not,

162
00:09:22,407 --> 00:09:24,877
has never been proven to this day.

163
00:09:24,877 --> 00:09:28,582
That's why we put it in the box of legend,

164
00:09:28,582 --> 00:09:30,640
not in the box of history.

165
00:09:30,640 --> 00:09:33,018
Now let's talk about the questions

166
00:09:33,018 --> 00:09:35,057
whose answers are still incomplete.

167
00:09:35,057 --> 00:09:38,794
Was the pillar hung on purpose, or did it slip later?

168
00:09:38,794 --> 00:09:41,172
The final verdict on this is still pending.

169
00:09:41,172 --> 00:09:44,570
And maybe only when new evidence comes will this knot be untied.

170
00:09:44,570 --> 00:09:46,023
There are more questions.

171
00:09:46,023 --> 00:09:50,383
How much sideways push this mandap's roof can withstand,

172
00:09:50,383 --> 00:09:54,380
has not been fully worked out. Sideways push means lateral load,

173
00:09:54,380 --> 00:09:58,740
which pushes the stone sideways in an earthquake or strong wind.

174
00:09:58,740 --> 00:10:02,255
That is, how much strength the Vijayanagara craftsmen achieved

175
00:10:02,255 --> 00:10:05,379
has still not been fully measured.

176
00:10:05,379 --> 00:10:07,722
Maybe future research will uncover it.

177
00:10:07,722 --> 00:10:11,628
Until then, this pillar stands as a question, or you could say,

178
00:10:11,628 --> 00:10:13,190
hangs as a question.

179
00:10:13,190 --> 00:10:17,037
Now something useful for you. If you want to visit Lepakshi,

180
00:10:17,037 --> 00:10:20,534
what should you see? This village is in Andhra Pradesh,

181
00:10:20,534 --> 00:10:22,983
and the temple is still worshipped today. If you go,

182
00:10:22,983 --> 00:10:26,480
keep full respect for the faith and the temple's rules.

183
00:10:26,480 --> 00:10:28,759
Go into the mandap and see the hanging pillar.

184
00:10:28,759 --> 00:10:32,176
People still pass a cloth under it even today,

185
00:10:32,176 --> 00:10:35,974
and consider it auspicious. It is a tradition of faith.

186
00:10:35,974 --> 00:10:40,151
Just make sure the old stone is not pushed or damaged,

187
00:10:40,151 --> 00:10:42,050
because this heritage belongs to everyone.

188
00:10:42,050 --> 00:10:44,932
Look for the eye-like marks of Virupanna on the wall.

189
00:10:44,932 --> 00:10:47,814
See the colourful paintings on the ceiling and pillars.

190
00:10:47,814 --> 00:10:52,498
And near the temple, be sure to see the huge Nandi statue made of granite,

191
00:10:52,498 --> 00:10:55,020
which can be seen from far away.

192
00:10:55,020 --> 00:10:58,918
So finally, what does the evidence say? The pillar is not in the air,

193
00:10:58,918 --> 00:11:00,336
but it almost hangs.

194
00:11:00,336 --> 00:11:03,880
The weight of the roof is shared across the network of pillars.

195
00:11:03,880 --> 00:11:07,424
And whether this was deliberate or the effect of time,

196
00:11:07,424 --> 00:11:09,550
is still an open question.

197
00:11:09,550 --> 00:11:13,783
Now a direct question for you. What do you think,

198
00:11:13,783 --> 00:11:16,958
was this pillar the craftsmen's intention,

199
00:11:16,958 --> 00:11:21,721
or the effect of the ground settling? Tell us in the comments.

200
00:11:21,721 --> 00:11:25,954
Next time we will go to Hampi, the capital of Vijayanagara,

201
00:11:25,954 --> 00:11:28,600
to find the secrets of its stones.
